export const pkUnit05CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 5: Food and Care — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 5: Food and Care helps children connect food, drink, hygiene, self-care, table routines, pretend kitchen play, and care for others. Compared with PG, PK children can participate in more intentional choosing, serving, washing, wiping, table setting, snack clean-up, and simple role play.\n\nThis unit remains non-language developmental work. Children are not required to produce full preference sentences, answer food questions accurately, or explain healthy eating. The focus is real-life care routines, sensory exploration, social participation, short request or response chunks, and safe handling of food-related materials.\n\nChildren may use single words or short chunks such as `water`, `apple`, `banana`, `more`, `all done`, `clean hands`, `thank you`, or `I want...` when ready, but action-based participation remains valid.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\nwater ｜ milk ｜ snack ｜ apple ｜ banana ｜ bread ｜ rice ｜ cookie ｜ cup ｜ spoon ｜ plate ｜ bowl ｜ napkin ｜ hands ｜ face ｜ mouth ｜ wash ｜ wipe ｜ clean ｜ dirty ｜ more ｜ all done ｜ hungry ｜ thirsty ｜ kitchen ｜ table\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nDrink water. ｜ Wash your hands. ｜ Wipe your mouth. ｜ Clean hands. ｜ Dirty hands. ｜ Snack time. ｜ Are you hungry? ｜ Are you thirsty? ｜ More? ｜ More, please. ｜ All done? ｜ I want water. ｜ I want apple. ｜ Give me your cup. ｜ Put it on the plate. ｜ Put the spoon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Water, Cup and Table Routine\n\nChildren revisit drink routines and expand into table setting. The focus is cup, water, milk, thirsty, table, plate, spoon, and putting objects in the right place.\n\n### Week 2: Snack, Fruit and Choice\n\nChildren explore familiar snack foods and choices through apple, banana, bread, rice, cookie, plate, and simple `more / all done` routines.\n\n### Week 3: Wash, Wipe, Clean and Dirty\n\nChildren connect food routines with hygiene. The focus is washing hands, wiping mouth or face, showing clean hands, identifying dirty/clean through action, and returning care materials.\n\n### Week 4: Kitchen, Care and Clean-Up\n\nChildren integrate food and care through pretend kitchen, serving, table setting, baby/teddy care, snack clean-up, and classroom helper routines.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Food and Drink Input\n\nwater ｜ milk ｜ snack ｜ apple ｜ banana ｜ bread ｜ rice ｜ cookie ｜ hungry ｜ thirsty ｜ more ｜ all done ｜ I want water ｜ I want apple ｜ more, please\n\n### Table and Object Input\n\ncup ｜ spoon ｜ plate ｜ bowl ｜ napkin ｜ table ｜ kitchen ｜ put it on the plate ｜ cup here ｜ spoon here ｜ plate here ｜ give me your cup\n\n### Hygiene and Care Input\n\nhands ｜ face ｜ mouth ｜ wash ｜ wipe ｜ clean ｜ dirty ｜ clean hands ｜ dirty hands ｜ wash your hands ｜ wipe your mouth ｜ show me your hands\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nSnack time. ｜ Are you hungry? ｜ Drink water. ｜ Wash your hands. ｜ Show me your hands. ｜ Wipe your mouth. ｜ Clean hands. ｜ More? ｜ All done? ｜ Put it on the plate. ｜ Put the spoon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by looking, pointing, choosing between food or drink options, naming familiar food or drink when ready, saying or indicating `more`, `all done`, or `water`, washing hands, wiping mouth, showing hands, putting food props on plates, placing cups or spoons in trays, serving a doll or friend, waiting for a turn, cleaning up table materials, or using short chunks such as `more, please`, `I want water`, or `thank you` when ready.\n\nChildren are not expected to produce full preference sentences consistently, answer all food questions verbally, explain healthy/unhealthy, eat unfamiliar foods as part of the curriculum task, or complete worksheet-based food classification.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nAre You Hungry  \nPeanut Butter & Jelly  \nPat-A-Cake  \nThe Bath Song  \nBrush Your Teeth  \nDo You Like Broccoli Ice Cream  \nDo You Like Spaghetti Yogurt  \nDo You Like Pickle Pudding  \nApples & Bananas  \nThis Is The Way\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should support snack readiness, table routines, washing, wiping, pretend cooking, stirring, patting, brushing, choosing, and clean-up. Children may sing repeated words when ready, but gesture, movement, object handling, washing, wiping, stirring, and serving remain valid participation.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nClassroom  \nCooking classroom  \nPretend kitchen / home corner  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nScience classroom for water and washing routines  \nArt classroom  \nMusic classroom  \nTable activity area  \nCircle-time area / mat area\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nCups  \nSpoons  \nPlates  \nBowls  \nNapkins  \nTowels  \nWet wipes  \nCleaning cloths  \nWater basins  \nWater tray  \nToy food  \nToy kitchen  \nShopping baskets  \nSnack mats  \nFruit props  \nReal fruit when appropriate  \nBaby dolls  \nTeddy bears  \nDoll care items  \nPuppets  \nBaskets / trays  \nVisual routine cards  \nAprons  \nToy sink  \nToy stove  \nWaterproof table covers\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nThe cooking classroom can support simple food preparation, table setting, and washing routines. The pretend kitchen can support symbolic food and care play. Water trays and basins can support washing and pouring. Art can support food prints, plate collage, and clean-hands routines. Dolls and teddies can support care for others through feeding, wiping, and offering water.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a food-preference speaking test. `I want...`, `I like...`, and `More, please` are supported chunks, not universal output requirements.\n\nThis unit should not require children to eat unfamiliar foods. All real food use must respect allergy, safety, family policy, and children’s sensory readiness.\n\nThis unit should not moralise food. Avoid complex healthy/unhealthy judgement tasks at PK level.\n\nThis unit should not treat hygiene as scolding. Clean/dirty should be experienced through calm care routines and supportive teacher language.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children participate in drink, snack, washing, wiping, table setting, serving, and clean-up routines; use or attempt food and care words; make simple choices; signal more/all done; wait for turns; care for dolls or peers through pretend serving; and handle cups, plates, spoons, cloths, and food props safely.\n\nObservation should focus on self-care participation, sensory comfort, routine independence, social care, safe material use, and readiness-based communication rather than full verbal food description.\n",
  "zh": "# PK Unit 5: Food and Care — Common Info｜PK 单元 5：食物与照护 — 通用信息\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview｜单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 5：食物与照护帮助儿童把食物、饮料、卫生、自我照护、餐桌常规、假装厨房游戏和照护他人连接起来。和 PG 相比，PK 儿童可以参与更有意识的选择、端菜、清洗、擦、摆桌、点心收拾和简单的角色游戏。\n\n本单元仍是非语言发展工作。不要求儿童产出完整偏好句、准确回答食物问题或解释健康饮食。重点是真实生活照护常规、感官探索、社交参与、简短请求或回应句块，以及安全处理食物相关材料。\n\n儿童在准备好时可以使用单个词或简短句块，例如 `water`、`apple`、`banana`、`more`、`all done`、`clean hands`、`thank you`、`I want...`，但基于动作的参与仍有效。\n\n## 2. Unit Language｜单元语言\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary｜PK 核心词汇\n\nwater ｜ milk ｜ snack ｜ apple ｜ banana ｜ bread ｜ rice ｜ cookie ｜ cup ｜ spoon ｜ plate ｜ bowl ｜ napkin ｜ hands ｜ face ｜ mouth ｜ wash ｜ wipe ｜ clean ｜ dirty ｜ more ｜ all done ｜ hungry ｜ thirsty ｜ kitchen ｜ table\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks｜实用教师句块\n\nDrink water. ｜ Wash your hands. ｜ Wipe your mouth. ｜ Clean hands. ｜ Dirty hands. ｜ Snack time. ｜ Are you hungry? ｜ Are you thirsty? ｜ More? ｜ More, please. ｜ All done? ｜ I want water. ｜ I want apple. ｜ Give me your cup. ｜ Put it on the plate. ｜ Put the spoon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn.\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes｜周次子主题\n\n### Week 1: Water, Cup and Table Routine｜第一周：水、杯子和餐桌常规\n\n儿童重新接触饮料常规并扩展到摆桌。重点是杯子、水、奶、渴、桌子、盘子、勺子以及把物品放到正确的位置。\n\n### Week 2: Snack, Fruit and Choice｜第二周：点心、水果与选择\n\n儿童通过苹果、香蕉、面包、米饭、饼干、盘子和简单的 `more / all done` 常规来探索熟悉的点心食物和选择。\n\n### Week 3: Wash, Wipe, Clean and Dirty｜第三周：清洗、擦、干净和脏\n\n儿童把食物常规和卫生连接起来。重点是洗手、擦嘴或脸、展示干净的手、通过动作识别脏／干净、以及归还照护材料。\n\n### Week 4: Kitchen, Care and Clean-Up｜第四周：厨房、照护与收拾\n\n儿童通过假装厨房、端菜、摆桌、宝宝／泰迪照护、点心收拾和班级帮手常规把食物和照护整合起来。\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool｜教师输入库\n\n### Food and Drink Input｜食物与饮料输入\n\nwater ｜ milk ｜ snack ｜ apple ｜ banana ｜ bread ｜ rice ｜ cookie ｜ hungry ｜ thirsty ｜ more ｜ all done ｜ I want water ｜ I want apple ｜ more, please\n\n### Table and Object Input｜餐桌与物品输入\n\ncup ｜ spoon ｜ plate ｜ bowl ｜ napkin ｜ table ｜ kitchen ｜ put it on the plate ｜ cup here ｜ spoon here ｜ plate here ｜ give me your cup\n\n### Hygiene and Care Input｜卫生与照护输入\n\nhands ｜ face ｜ mouth ｜ wash ｜ wipe ｜ clean ｜ dirty ｜ clean hands ｜ dirty hands ｜ wash your hands ｜ wipe your mouth ｜ show me your hands\n\n### Teacher Routine Language｜教师常规语言\n\nSnack time. ｜ Are you hungry? ｜ Drink water. ｜ Wash your hands. ｜ Show me your hands. ｜ Wipe your mouth. ｜ Clean hands. ｜ More? ｜ All done? ｜ Put it on the plate. ｜ Put the spoon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes｜预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：看、指、在食物或饮料选项间选择、在准备好时命名熟悉的食物或饮料、说或示意 `more`、`all done` 或 `water`、洗手、擦嘴、展示手、把食物道具放到盘子上、把杯子或勺子放进托盘、给一个娃娃或朋友端菜、等待一次轮流、收拾餐桌材料，或在准备好时使用简短句块，例如 `more, please`、`I want water`、`thank you`。\n\n不要求儿童一致产出完整偏好句、口头回答所有食物问题、解释健康／不健康、吃不熟悉的食物作为课程任务的一部分，或完成基于练习册的食物分类。\n\n## 6. Core Unit Songs｜单元核心歌曲\n\n### Core Songs｜核心歌曲\n\nAre You Hungry  \nPeanut Butter & Jelly  \nPat-A-Cake  \nThe Bath Song  \nBrush Your Teeth  \nDo You Like Broccoli Ice Cream  \nDo You Like Spaghetti Yogurt  \nDo You Like Pickle Pudding  \nApples & Bananas  \nThis Is The Way\n\n### Use of Songs｜歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当支持点心准备度、餐桌常规、清洗、擦、假装烹饪、搅拌、轻拍、刷、选择和收拾。儿童在准备好时可以唱重复词，但手势、动作、物品处理、清洗、擦、搅拌和端菜仍是有效的参与。\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities｜资源机会\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces｜可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nClassroom  \nCooking classroom  \nPretend kitchen / home corner  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nScience classroom for water and washing routines  \nArt classroom  \nMusic classroom  \nTable activity area  \nCircle-time area / mat area\n\n### Useful Materials｜可用材料\n\nCups  \nSpoons  \nPlates  \nBowls  \nNapkins  \nTowels  \nWet wipes  \nCleaning cloths  \nWater basins  \nWater tray  \nToy food  \nToy kitchen  \nShopping baskets  \nSnack mats  \nFruit props  \nReal fruit when appropriate  \nBaby dolls  \nTeddy bears  \nDoll care items  \nPuppets  \nBaskets / trays  \nVisual routine cards  \nAprons  \nToy sink  \nToy stove  \nWaterproof table covers\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities｜拓展资源机会\n\n烹饪教室可以支持简单的食物准备、摆桌和清洗常规。假装厨房可以支持象征性的食物和照护游戏。水盘和水盆可以支持清洗和倾倒。艺术可以支持食物印、盘子拼贴和干净的手的常规。娃娃和泰迪可以通过喂食、擦和给水支持照护他人。\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes｜边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成食物偏好的口语测试。\"I want...\"、\"I like...\" 和 \"More, please\" 是支持的句块，而非普遍输出要求。\n\n本单元不应要求儿童吃不熟悉的食物。所有真实食物的使用必须尊重过敏、安全、家庭政策和儿童的感官准备度。\n\n本单元不应让食物道德化。避免在 PK 层级使用复杂的健康／不健康判断任务。\n\n本单元不应把卫生当作指责。干净／脏应通过平静的照护常规和支持性的教师语言来体验。\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus｜单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否参与喝、点心、清洗、擦、摆桌、端菜和收拾的常规；使用或尝试食物和照护词；做出简单的选择；示意 more／all done；等待轮流；通过假装端菜照护娃娃或同伴；以及安全地处理杯子、盘子、勺子、布和食物道具。\n\n观察应聚焦于自我照护参与、感官舒适度、常规独立性、社交照护、安全材料使用和准备度导向的交流，而非完整口头食物描述。"
};

